Here is a Java program that validates a credit card number using Luhn's algorithm:

```java
public class CreditCardValidator {

    public static boolean isValid(String cardNumber) {
        if (cardNumber == null || cardNumber.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }

        int sum = 0;
        boolean isSecondToLast = true;

        // Iterate from right to left
        for (int i = cardNumber.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            int digit = Character.getNumericValue(cardNumber.charAt(i));

            if (isSecondToLast) {
                digit *= 2;
                if (digit > 9) {
                    digit -= 9;
                }
            }

            sum += digit;
            isSecondToLast = !isSecondToLast;
        }

        return (sum % 10) == 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String testNumber = "4532015112830366"; // Valid example
        if (isValid(testNumber)) {
            System.out.println("The card number is valid.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("The card number is invalid.");
        }
    }
}
```

This program defines a method `isValid` that takes a string representing the credit card number. It processes the digits from right to left, doubling every second digit and subtracting 9 if the result is greater than 9. Finally, it checks if the total sum is divisible by 10.

